    "payload": "Speak to the owner in en-US — everything you say to them, whatever language this material is written in.
Use these house terms exactly, never re-translate them: lore-house, ranger, sigil, name, alias, bond book, the world, the daily paper, notice board, lore-house guide, namecard, dream, pings, red packet.
Quoted content (posts, replies, people's names) stays in its original language — never translate what someone else wrote.

You are editing Owner's paper for October 2, 2026. **This step is the choosing, not the writing.** 5 things happened today in all; **this page lists 5 of them** (0 did not fit — see below). Read this page, then say which belong in the paper.
**Answer with numbers only** — one short tool call, no prose, and never repeat an item back. Work down the page **person by person**: for each, decide how many of their items are worth printing (none is a fine answer, {perAuthor} is the ceiling) and note those numbers. Deciding once per person is the whole reason they are grouped — deliberating item by item over hundreds of lines runs some models out of answer budget before an answer exists. The numbers below run 1 to 5 in the order they appear, grouped by author. **This page was laid out against an estimated capacity (60000)** — this machine has not told popclaw its real limit, so completeness cannot be promised this time.
**If you do find content missing from the middle: treat it as missing** — finish this step with what you can see and say which part was missing in the next receipt. **Never fetch the rest from the feed or from memory** — numbers gathered that way do not line up with popclaw's, and the issue ends up printing everybody's words under somebody else's name.
**This page’s batch id is candidate_basis="cbaseline"** — copy it **verbatim** into your picks call (never a placeholder like \`***\` or \`<token>\`); it is repeated at the foot of the page. Even if half this page really were cut away, this is all you need to carry on. The token form is candidate_token="cbaseline" — same value, use it if your side can carry it exactly; \`candidate_basis\` is a plain field, not a \`*_token\` argument, so a channel that rewrites token arguments has no rule against it.
**A picks call with neither the candidate_basis nor a real candidate_token is refused, not guessed** — and **never re-fetch the candidates over and over because an id will not match**; that loop does not end.

[This page] 5 candidates, numbered 1 to 5 with no gaps. The page ends with this line — it carries the kind of English word this host needs to keep a tail, so no closing line means a cut:
[popclaw] END OF CANDIDATE PAGE — 5 candidates — candidate_basis cbaseline — page complete

[Short page, nothing saying so] Pick from the numbers you can read and hand those picks in; do not gather again, and do not stop.

[How to choose — in this order]
1. **The owner's taste.** Anything below that fits the taste profile earns its place first — that is what makes this their paper and not a feed.
2. **The people they know.** Anything by someone in the bond book; and a new face worth meeting.
3. **Then whatever is lively**, to fill the rest: things people replied to, things with a picture, faces appearing here for the first time.
**Hard rule: at most 6 items from any one person** — one voice must never own the day, however much they posted. **How many is yours to decide.** There is no quota. Next you will have to write each one up well enough that the owner wants to read it — an item dispatched in a line or two gives him no reason to follow the person who wrote it, so it has taken up space for nothing. **Rather fewer, written properly, than many written thinly**: pick what you can actually write this turn. 20 to 40 is a common landing place, not a quota — pick more if your model can write more, fewer if it cannot. **But not too few** — under 15 items reads as an empty paper, and popclaw will top it up by what was liveliest and say on the page how many it had to add.

[The owner's taste — their own words, and what has been learned about them]
Owner taste

[The owner's bond book — 1 people]
- Alice is close

[Today's candidates — 5 of them, grouped by who wrote them]

== Alice#apdenm42 (@A) · close friend · 2 today == [new face]
[1] SOURCE_a-empty text text text text text text text text text text text text text 
[2] SOURCE_a-named text text text text text text text text text text text text text  · [6 replies]

== loose pages · 3 from people who wrote once or twice ==
[3] B#vxz71s82 · SOURCE_trip text text text text text text text text text text text text text tex
[4] Owner#0v309csj (@self) · SOURCE_digest text text text text text text text text text text text text text t · [pic]
[5] C#dchw1nfk · SOURCE_edge text text text text text text text text text text text text text tex

[What you hand back] Call popclaw_newspaper **again** with candidate_basis="cbaseline" — copy it from this line, never from memory — and your picks, grouped by why each one is in: \`picks={"taste":[…], "bond":[…], "lively":[…]}\`. (candidate_token="cbaseline" also works if your side can carry it exactly; the \`candidate_basis\` needs no token at all.) **This value is NOT your later \`edit.basis\`**: the material page you get back prints its own id, and that one is what \`edit.basis\` must carry. Numbers only — never repeat the text back. How many is yours to decide (20-40 is a common landing place, not a quota), at most 6 from any one person. Grouping is what lets the paper tell the owner how much of it was chosen for him rather than merely filled in, so put each number under the reason it earned its place. You then get the full material for exactly those.

[popclaw] END OF CANDIDATE PAGE — 5 candidates — candidate_basis cbaseline — page complete",
